summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Follow-up of chronic problems.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Carolyn Brooks is a 49-year-old female who presents today for follow-up of her chronic problems. She is doing okay. She states her osteoarthritis has been flaring up lately, mostly in her elbows, and it has been bothering her. She has been sitting at her desk at the office more, so she is not sure if that has increased flare-ups for that. She wanted to talk to me about that.

She is right-handed. Her right elbow bothers her more than the other, but primarily she is typing all day versus writing, so it flares up in both. She states she has some numbing in her hands when she is typing for long periods of time. She tries to shake out her arms a little bit to relieve that sensation.

She has a history of gout. Her last episode was 3 months ago. She had some inflammation of her toe. She has not had any other issues with that. She states the medication I gave her really controlled it, and she has not seen a flare-up since.

She is doing well with her psoriasis. She states that has not been a major problem for her either.

She is doing well with her clobetasol scalp.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Denies fever, chills, or weight changes.
• Musculoskeletal: Reports joint pain, right elbow pain, and stiffness. Reports right elbow swelling. Reports left elbow pain and stiffness.
• Neurological: Reports bilateral hand numbness.

VITALS

Looking good.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• Constitutional: No cervical lymphadenopathy.
• Neck: Supple without thyromegaly.
• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.
• Musculoskeletal: Examination of the right elbow: Edema and inflammation of the right olecranon. Tenderness and effusion. Pain to palpation of the olecranon bursa. Pain with pronation and supination. Pain with flexion and extension of the right arm. Examination of the left elbow: No edema or inflammation. No pain to palpation. No pain with flexion and extension.

RESULTS

X-rays of the right elbow.

ASSESSMENT

• Gout.
• Osteoarthritis, right elbow.
• Psoriasis.

MEDICATIONS

Clobetasol.

PLAN

I have discussed my impression with the patient. For her gout, we will continue her on colchicine 0.6 mg twice a day as needed for flare-ups. For her osteoarthritis, I have prescribed meloxicam 15 mg once a day for the pain and swelling. I would like to see the results of the x-ray before referring her for any type of physical therapy. From a psoriasis standpoint, everything is stable. We will continue her on the steroid cream for her scalp.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>
