Back pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Ms. Lee is a 40-year-old female who presents today for the evaluation of back pain. The onset of her pain started 2 days ago after a fall at a birthday party. The patient was at a surprise party and was hiding behind someone. When the person with the gift came into the room, she and the other guests jumped out and the patient fell down the stairs, ramming her back into the railing of the staircase. She believes her pain was from the jump and twisting motion.

The patient reports that her pain is worse when lying flat. She denies any pain shooting down her legs. She states that her pain is more on the right side. The patient has a history of lumbar fusion.

MEDICAL HISTORY

The patient reports a history of lumbar fusion.

SOCIAL HISTORY

The patient reports that she attends birthday parties.

MEDICATIONS

The patient reports taking ibuprofen.

PAST MEDICAL HISTORY

The patient reports a history of anxiety.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports back pain.

VITALS

The patient is afebrile.

PHYSICAL EXAM

MSK: Examination of the musculoskeletal system is unremarkable. No tenderness or pain to palpation of the lumbar spine. No pain with palpation of the paraspinal muscles. No pain with extension or flexion of the back. No pain with straight leg raise. No pain with seated straight leg raise.

RESULTS

X-rays of the lumbar spine show fusion at the T4 level. No evidence of any compression fractures.

ASSESSMENT

1. Strain of the lumbar spine.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to her current symptoms. She is going to continue taking Motrin 800 mg 3 times a day with food. I want her to use ice and heat alternately on her back. She should ambulate. If her pain worsens or does not improve over the next week, she should follow up with me.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in one week if her symptoms do not improve. }}<|eot_id|>
