Yearly follow-up.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

The patient is a 20-year-old female who presents for a yearly follow-up for asthma, allergic rhinitis, and food allergy.

The patient reports that she has been doing well with her asthma since our last office visit in 04/2020. She was switched from Symbicort to Wixela due to insurance changes. She uses 1 puff in the morning and 1 puff at night and reports that it has been working well. She uses her albuterol inhaler rarely, and only if she is exercising. She notes that she has been trying to use her daily inhaler less as she is trying to get into the military soon. The patient reports that she has not had any urgent care or ER visits for breathing over the past year.

The patient reports that she has been taking Zyrtec in the spring, summer, and fall for her seasonal allergy symptoms, which tend to work well. She notes that she takes it on days when she knows she will be outside a lot. She does work outside 3 days a week. The patient has known peanut allergy, she tolerates almonds and cashews. Written consent was obtained to use DAX for documentation.

The patient reports that she was given a breathing test today for the military. She notes that she is trying to get into the military soon and is trying to show that she can operate without her inhaler. She notes that she has been trying to use her inhaler less.

PAST HISTORY

Medical
Asthma.
Allergic Rhinitis.
Peanut Allergy.

SOCIAL HISTORY

The patient reports that she has been trying to cut back on vaping and smoking, but has not been successful. She notes that she only does it occasionally now and is trying to quit.

FAMILY HISTORY

The patient reports that she has a family history of asthma.

MEDICATIONS

The patient reports that she is currently using Symbicort, Wixela, albuterol inhaler, and Zyrtec.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or weight loss.
Eyes: Denies conjunctival redness or itchiness.
Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath or cough.
Skin: Denies hives or rash.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Ears, Nose, Mouth, and Throat: No abnormalities noted.
Lungs: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.

RESULTS

Spirometry: Spirometry performed today shows an FEV1 of 1.8 liters, which is a 129% of predicted. This is similar to her results from 04/2019.

ASSESSMENT

• Asthma.
• Allergic rhinitis.
• Peanut allergy.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's test results and discussing her current symptoms, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient. I have recommended that she continue to use her Wixela twice a day. I have also recommended that she try to step down to an inhaler that has one med, Alvesco. I have ordered a refill of her albuterol inhaler and her epipen. I have also recommended that she continue to use her Zyrtec as needed. I have recommended that she continue to avoid peanuts and tree nuts. I have also recommended that she repeat her breathing test in 4 months to see how she is doing.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will continue to use her Wixela twice a day. She will try to step down to an inhaler that has one med, Alvesco. She will continue to use her albuterol inhaler as needed. She will continue to use her Zyrtec as needed. She will continue to avoid peanuts and tree nuts. She will repeat her breathing test in 4 months to see how she is doing. }}<|eot_id|>
