Ms. Rodriguez has a history of hypertension and diabetes. She is currently taking lisinopril 10 mg daily and metformin 500 mg twice daily.

SOCIAL HISTORY

Ms. Rodriguez reports that she enjoys pottery and baking. She is a sweet tooth. She has been baking since she was young and enjoys making cakes and cinnamon rolls. She has been making a ceramic bowl for her son's birthday and has been working on it a lot. She reports that she is a busy person and has been doing a lot of walking in the neighborhood.

MEDICATIONS

The patient reports that she is compliant with taking her medications.

VITALS

Blood pressure: 120/70 mmHg.
Respiratory rate: 18 breaths per minute.
Oxygen saturation: 100%.
Pulse: regular, 80 beats per minute.

PHYSICAL EXAM

CV: No murmurs, rubs, or gallops.
Respiratory: Clear bilaterally to auscultation.
MSK: Examination of the right elbow reveals moderate tenderness at the medial epicondyle. Pain with resisted pronation of the right forearm. Pain with flexion and extension of the wrist. Pain when turning the wrist inward. No pain when turning the wrist outward. No bruising or swelling. No discoloration or lesions. Equal pulses bilaterally.

RESULTS

X-ray of the right elbow reveals no bony abnormalities.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

1. Right elbow pain.
• Medical epicondylitis.
- Patient will rest her right arm.
- Ice to the elbow for 20 minutes 3 times a day.
- She will take 600 mg of ibuprofen 3 times a day for 1 week.
- She will hold off on pottery for the next couple of weeks.

2. Diabetes.
- Medical Reasoning: The patient has been having a hard time keeping up with her metformin.
- Medical Treatment: Increase metformin to 1000 mg twice daily.
- Patient Education and Counseling: I recommended a referral to diabetic counseling.

3. Hypertension.
- Medical Reasoning: The patient's blood pressure is well controlled.
- Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 10 mg daily.
- Patient Education and Counseling: None.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>
