summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Nonhealing right foot ulcer.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Christina Davis is a pleasant 44-year-old female who presents today for evaluation of a nonhealing right foot ulcer. She states that she has had this ulcer for approximately one year. She denies any trauma to the foot. The patient states that she had a blister on her foot and then she picked at it and it became red and infected. She was placed on antibiotics by her primary care physician and it seemed to improve, but nothing has really made it go away. The patient states that she has been on Keflex for the past five months. She denies any pain in the foot but does not have much feeling in her foot. She reports that she has been more tired lately and there has been a lot more drainage.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Reports fatigue.
• Musculoskeletal: Reports right foot pain.

VITALS

Vitals are stable.

PHYSICAL EXAM

MSK: Examination of the right foot: There is a 2 cm x 1 cm circular wound on the dorsal aspect of the lateral right foot, proximal to the 5th MTP joint. There is yellow slough present with minimal granulation tissue. There is no surrounding erythema or cellulitis. There is no evidence of fluid collection, necrosis, or bony exposure. There is no odor. There is no pain to palpation.

RESULTS

X-ray of the right foot taken today shows no evidence of osteomyelitis.

ASSESSMENT

Diabetic foot ulcer.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to her current symptoms. We discussed her current treatment plan and the fact that her wound has not been healing. She is currently taking Keflex, which was prescribed by her primary care physician. She has been on this medication for five months. We have discussed that we will continue to treat her wound with debridement and colaganase ointment. We will have her return in two weeks to check on the wound and possibly perform another debridement.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in two weeks. She will also follow up with her primary care physician regarding her diabetes. She will be placed in a surgical shoe and will be instructed to avoid putting pressure on the area. }}<|eot_id|>
