summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Follow up after interior STEMI.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

The patient is a 61-year-old female who presents for follow-up after recent inferior STEMI. She reports that she had been doing well until she started having chest pain again. She was seen in the emergency room where she was found to have a new stent. She denies chest pain or shortness of breath since her last visit 6 months ago. She reports that she has been walking her dog daily and walking about 1.5 miles to 2 miles. She is excited for the fall season and has holiday plans. She has a history of coronary artery disease, hypertension, and diabetes.

The patient has been compliant with her medications. She reports that she has been able to afford her medications. She denies leg swelling, lying flat at night, and she is able to take her medications as prescribed. She reports that she has not been watching her salt intake since her last heart attack. She states that she has been trying to watch her sugar intake. She reports that she is trying to stay active and walks her dog daily. She reports that she is not on insulin and is currently taking metformin.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Cardiovascular: Reports chest pain.
• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.
• Musculoskeletal: Denies leg swelling.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• Neck
	- General Examination: No jugular venous distention.
	- Carotid: No bruits.
• Respiratory
	- Auscultation of Lungs: Clear bilaterally.
• Cardiovascular
	- Auscultation of Heart: Grade 3/6 systolic ejection murmur heard at the left base.
• Musculoskeletal
	- Examination: Lower extremities: 1+ pitting edema of the right leg.
• Extremities
	- Examination: Right radial artery pulses are palpable. Clean, dry, and intact cath site.

RESULTS

Electrocardiogram shows normal sinus rhythm, good R wave progression, and evolutionary changes anticipated after STEMI.

Echocardiogram reveals reduced left ventricular ejection fraction of 35%. There is moderate mitral regurgitation.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Coronary artery disease.
• Medical Reasoning: She has been compliant with her medications.
• Medical Treatment: Continue aspirin 81 mg daily. Continue Brilinta 90 mg twice daily. Continue high-dose statin Lipitor 80 mg daily. Continue Toprol 50 mg daily.

Reduced left ventricular dysfunction and moderate mitral regurgitation.
• Medical Reasoning: She was seen quickly after her STEMI and has been compliant with her medications.
• Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 20 mg daily.

Acute heart failure.
• Medical Reasoning: She has been compliant with her medications and has been trying to watch her salt intake.
• Medical Treatment: We will start her on a diuretic Lasix 40 mg daily. Add Aldactone 12.5 mg daily.

Hypertension.
• Medical Reasoning: She has been compliant with her medications.
• Medical Treatment: She should get nutrition counseling to consider dietary modifications.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.
INSTRUCTIONS

Repeat echocardiogram in 2 months. }}<|eot_id|>
