summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Abdominal pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Barbara Rodriguez is a 31-year-old female who presents today for abdominal pain. The onset of her pain began one week ago and is gradually increasing in severity. She has a longstanding history of diabetes type 2 and asthma. She has been drinking a lot of coffee lately, which she believes may have triggered her gastritis. The patient has had nausea and vomiting approximately 3 times in the past week. She has vomited a couple of times. She has been drinking a lot of coffee and believes this may be related to her pain. She believes that her diabetes has been under control, and her blood sugars have been in the 800s. She has been taking metformin 500 mg once a day, which was increased to 500 mg twice a day at her last visit. She has had some recent exercise-induced pain in her abdomen. She has been jogging or going for a run and her abdominal pain spikes. She has been told to take it easy. She has not had any fever.

PAST HISTORY

Medical
Diabetes type 2.
Asthma.

SOCIAL HISTORY

Patient reports that she has been drinking a lot of coffee lately. She has also been drinking alcohol.

MEDICATIONS

Metformin 500 mg twice a day.
Flovent 50 mcg twice a day.
Albuterol as needed.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Negative for fever.
Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain.
Gastrointestinal: Endorses abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting.
Respiratory: Endorses mild end expiratory wheezes.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Respiratory
Lung exam: Mild end expiratory wheezes, otherwise normal lung exam with excellent air movement.

Cardiovascular
Grade 3 out of 6 systolic ejection murmur, unchanged from prior exam.

Gastrointestinal
Mild tenderness in the epigastrium of the abdomen. Abdomen is otherwise soft. No tenderness in the right lower quadrant. No significant tenderness in the right upper quadrant. This is where the stomach is located.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

1. Acute gastritis.
• Medical Reasoning: I believe that her symptoms are most likely due to gastritis. She has a history of alcohol and caffeine intake, which may be exacerbating her symptoms.
• Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient to cut back on her alcohol intake to less than 3 drinks per week. I also advised her to cut back on her caffeine intake.
• Medical Treatment: I have prescribed the patient Zantac 150 mg twice a day to help with the acid in her stomach and the pain.

2. Diabetes type 2.
