summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Elevated cholesterol.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Julie Cruz is a 17-year-old non-binary, immunocompetent male who presents for the evaluation of newly diagnosed hypercholesterolemia. The patient was last seen in clinic in 2020 for annual checkup. The patient reports that he is concerned about the recent finding of elevated cholesterol. He denies a family history of hypercholesterolemia.

The patient states he enjoys outdoor activities and rides his bicycle frequently. He reports that he is active throughout the week. He is particularly fond of chocolate chip cookies. He denies any recent changes in his dietary habits.

The patient reports that he is currently living on his own and denies any exposure to secondhand smoke. He denies that any of his friends smoke. He reports that he was previously living with parents who smoked.

The patient reports that his grandparents have a history of hypercholesterolemia. He reports that one of his cousins died at the age of 44 or 45 due to a heart attack.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Cardiovascular: Negative for chest pain or dyspnea.
• Musculoskeletal: Positive for joint pain.

VITALS

Blood pressure is within normal limits.

PHYSICAL EXAM

CV: Auscultation of the heart: No murmurs, gallops, or rubs.
Respiratory: Auscultation of the lungs: Clear bilaterally.
Eyes: No exophthalmos, no exophthalmos.
Neck: No thyroid enlargement.

Gastrointestinal: No hepatomegaly or splenomegaly.

RESULTS

Cholesterol level was elevated at 210.

ASSESSMENT

• Hypercholesterolemia.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and test results, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regard to the diagnosis of hypercholesterolemia. The patient is currently asymptomatic. We discussed the etiology and pathophysiology of this condition and the importance of lifestyle modification. The patient was advised that the American Academy of Pediatrics recommends that all children be screened once between the ages of 9 and 11 years and again between 17 and 20 years. Studies have shown a link between high cholesterol and premature heart attacks.
We discussed the importance of maintaining a well-balanced diet. The patient will meet with a nutritionist who will provide information on what foods to eat. I will order a full cholesterol panel, complete metabolic panel, thyroid studies, and liver panels. We will also perform genetic testing to see if this problem is familial or secondary. I will follow up with a repeat cholesterol level in 3 to 6 months after the patient has met with the nutritionist and instituted dietary changes.
In addition, I advised the patient to continue being active, as he is currently doing well with this.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in 3 to 6 months. }}<|eot_id|>
