summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Bilateral knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Sophia Davis is a pleasant 29-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of bilateral knee pain. She states that she has been experiencing pain in both knees for approximately 6 months. The patient denies any specific injury that caused the pain. The patient describes the pain as a deep, achy pain that is felt behind her kneecaps. The patient notes that she can hear what seems like crepitus in her knees. The patient states that her pain is worse when she goes from a seated position to standing. The patient explains that she works from home and spends a lot of time at her desk. She also notes that her pain is worse when she watches TV or when she goes up and down stairs. The patient states that her pain improves after she rests for some time. The patient denies any recent trauma to her knees. She denies any recent illness or infection. The patient has tried applying ice, applying an ACE wrap, and taking ibuprofen or Aleve. The patient states that she tries to avoid taking any medications. The patient is an avid runner and has been running 5 to 6 miles a day. She notes that she can only get to half a mile now before her leg and knee start to hurt. She usually runs on the treadmill during the winter. The patient states that she prefers to run outdoors. She notes that she has never broken a bone.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports bilateral knee pain.
Skin: Denies erythema or ecchymosis.

VITALS

All vital signs are within normal limits.

PHYSICAL EXAM

CV: Auscultation of heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs.

MSK: Examination of the lower extremities: Strength is 3/5 for abduction of legs bilaterally. The remainder of muscle strength for the lower extremities is 5/5. Gait is normal. Negative Lachman, anterior and posterior drawer, and McMurray tests bilaterally. Tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinaculum. Positive patellar grind test. No erythema, ecchymosis, or warmth of the skin appreciated. No effusion.

RESULTS

X-rays of the bilateral knees were obtained and reviewed in the office today. The x-rays showed no fractures or osteoarthritis.

ASSESSMENT

Bilateral knee pain.

DIAGNOSIS

Patellofemoral pain syndrome.

PLAN

I have discussed the patient's diagnosis with her today. We discussed treatment options and she is going to continue to take ibuprofen or anti-inflammatory as needed to help with her pain. We discussed physical therapy and she will be starting that as well. She will be followed up in the clinic soon.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in the clinic soon. She will continue to take ibuprofen or anti-inflammatory as needed to help with her pain. She will start physical therapy. }}<|eot_id|>
