summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Low back pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Kyle Johnson is a pleasant 30-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of low back pain. The onset of his pain began 2 weeks ago when he felt a “pop” in his low back while lifting a box of books. He denies proper lifting technique. The pain is achy and sharp in nature. He initially did not have any pain down his left leg, but now is starting to feel pain shooting down his left leg. He states that sitting is usually okay for him. He denies numbness, tingling, loss of sensation, or weakness. He denies loss of bladder or bowel control. He has tried taking ibuprofen and Tylenol which sometimes helps. He denies any previous discussions of surgical options.

SOCIAL HISTORY

Patient reports that he is an Olympic weightlifter with books. He is inconsistent with his exercise routine. He works a sedentary job. He denies smoking. He reports that his father has back pain. He reports that he played soccer in high school. He is a Tottenham Hotspur fan.

FAMILY HISTORY

Patient reports that his father has back pain.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Musculoskeletal: Reports low back pain. Denies left leg pain.
• Neurological: Denies numbness, tingling, loss of sensation, or weakness.

VITALS

BP: 132/70
RR: 18

PHYSICAL EXAM

CV: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs.

MSK: Examination of the back: No overlying erythema or bruising on the skin. Midline tenderness at the L4-L5 disk space with right-sided lumbar paravertebral tenderness. Pain with lumbar flexion. Pain with extension. Supine straight leg test is positive.

NEURO: Normal strength and sensation in all extremities.

RESULTS

X-rays and MRI of the lumbar spine obtained and reviewed today demonstrate a disk desiccation, disk bulge with paracentral disk herniation resulting in moderate right neuroforaminal stenosis.

ASSESSMENT

• Lumbar back pain at the L4-L5 disk space due to a herniated disk with radiculopathy.

PLAN

I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms and the nature of his diagnosis. I have explained to him that his symptoms are in line with a lumbar back pain at the L4-L5 disk space due to a herniated disk with radiculopathy. This means that there is a bulge in one of the disks between the vertebrae that is pinching on some nerves, which is why he is feeling that pain radiating down his leg. I have recommended a spinal injection with a strong NSAID and corticosteroid to be performed under fluoroscopy to help with the inflammation that is causing his pain. He is a candidate for this procedure and we can get him scheduled today. All questions were answered and the patient is agreeable to proceed. }}<|eot_id|>
